* 0016-avfoundation-timebase-rate-servo:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 20:07:53 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] fix(avfoundation): slew the media timebase instead of
     snapping on drift

    mpv schedules video against its playback clock (the audio clock), while
    the CMTimebase driving AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer presentation runs on
    the CoreMedia host clock. On real hardware those clocks skew and wander.
    A fixed-rate timebase therefore accumulates phase error, while correcting
    that error with a hard re-anchor repeats, skips, or drops a queued frame.
    That is the recurring micro-stutter of plezy#1776.

    Replace the snap-only correction with a bounded PI servo. The
    proportional term retires phase error over a 2s horizon; the integral term
    absorbs constant clock skew. Corrections are capped at +-1.5%, rate writes
    are throttled, and >50ms discontinuities still re-anchor promptly.

    Do not infer playback speed from frame presentation deadlines. tvOS
    compressed-audio playback produces bursty deadlines (observed frame gaps
    from 34ms to 70ms at a constant 1x rate); the old inference treated those
    as speed changes and hard re-anchored repeatedly. Estimate nominal speed
    from media-PTS deltas divided by mpv's speed-adjusted frame durations,
    averaged over 500ms to remove timestamp quantization. Two coherent
    samples fast-track large real speed changes. Scheduling discontinuities
    no longer overwrite the nominal rate.

    Keep the servo and rate estimator freestanding so the simulation harness
    compiles the production code verbatim. It covers clock skew and wander,
    route-change steps, speed changes, timestamp jitter, and recurring 30ms
    deadline bursts. The burst trace produces 823 spurious anchors in the old
    model and zero with frame-duration rate estimation.

    The 5s pacing diagnostics report rate updates, applied offset in ppm, and
    the window's peak phase error.

* 0017-avfoundation-keep-displayed-frame-on-seek:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 15:49:37 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] fix(avfoundation): keep the displayed frame across seek
     resets

    Every seek fires VOCTRL_RESET (vo_seek_reset()), which the VO answered
    with flushAndRemoveImage on the display layer. Removing the displayed
    image blanks the AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer to black until the first
    post-seek frame makes it through demux and decode, so every skip
    flashed black for the seek latency -- glaring on tvOS where seeks ride
    the network (plezy#1803). Reference VOs (gpu/gpu-next) hold the last
    rendered frame through a seek.

    Flush without removal instead: queued-but-undisplayed samples are still
    discarded, but the on-screen frame holds until the first frame at the
    new position re-anchors the timebase and replaces it. The OSD layer
    keeps removing its image so a stale subtitle doesn't linger over the
    held frame, and uninit still removes the image on teardown.

* 0018-avfoundation-refuse-geometry-render-for-dolby-vision:

    From: Edvard Wikhall <edvard@wikhall.com>
    Date: Sun, 10 Aug 2026 14:20:00 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] fix(avfoundation): refuse geometry rendering for Dolby
     Vision passthrough frames

    Nonzero --video-zoom/--video-pan-* switch the VO to a per-frame Core
    Image re-render. Apple Dolby Vision passthrough buffers (native dvh1
    decode sessions) carry per-frame RPU metadata that only survives an
    untouched enqueue into AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer; re-rendered frames
    feed the DV display pipeline data the RPU no longer describes and the
    picture collapses to near-black (observed on tvOS in DV-Lowlatency
    HDMI mode).

    Skip geometry rendering for buffers carrying DolbyVisionRPUData and
    warn once: an unzoomed but correct picture beats a destroyed one.
    Hosts that need viewer zoom on this VO should scale the display layer
    instead.

