* 0030-avfoundation-hdr10plus-per-frame-metadata:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 23:50:00 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: attach HDR10+ per-frame metadata to display samples

    Companion to the ffmpeg patch that forwards HEVC prefix SEIs to
    VideoToolbox (plezy#2404). HEVC carries HDR10+ (SMPTE ST 2094-40) as a
    per-frame T.35 user-data SEI; FFmpeg parses it into
    AV_FRAME_DATA_DYNAMIC_HDR_PLUS on every decoded frame, hwaccel included,
    and mpv keeps that side data on the mp_image. It travels no further on its
    own: Core Video only has attachment keys for the static HDR10 metadata.

    AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer accepts HDR10+ from one documented place, the
    per-sample attachment kCMSampleAttachmentKey_HDR10PlusPerFrameData: the
    SEI payload as sent, starting at itu_t_t35_country_code, which overrides
    whatever the decoder attached to the pixel buffer. Serialize the parsed
    metadata back with av_dynamic_hdr_plus_to_t35 into a per-VO scratch and
    set it on each enqueued video sample. Applies to the geometry and
    composite render outputs as well: those keep the frame's PQ tags and the
    metadata describes the scene, not the pixel grid.

    Dolby Vision frames are skipped - their RPU is the dynamic metadata the DV
    pipeline presents - as are non-PQ transfers, and a pixel buffer that
    already carries DolbyVisionRPUData. One-shot info log on the first attached
    frame; one-shot warning when the OS is too old (pre-16) or the payload
    cannot be serialized, in which case playback continues as HDR10.

* 0032-avfoundation-render-subtitles-through-the-shared-osd-pipeline:

    avfoundation: render subtitles through the shared osd_ahead pipeline

    On Apple TV a subtitle frame that took longer than the queue lead to render
    made the video late with it: flip_page rendered the frame's subtitles on the
    VO thread and enqueued the video sample only afterwards. A heavy sign (the
    Ex2 sample, 105-122 ms on an Apple TV 4K) cost a late video frame every
    time it appeared.

    Render them on the shared render-ahead pipeline (osd_ahead.[ch], as
    vo_mediacodec does): draw_frame files the frame's request as soon as the
    frame is known, the pipeline pre-renders the frame its cadence predicts
    next and, while the OSD is static, the next subtitle event on a separate
    renderer (0013), and flip_page presents the result. flip_page waits for it
    only as avf_osd_wait_until allows: a timed frame keeps half its queue lead
    for the video, and does not wait at all while the render thread is still
    on an older frame; subtitles that arrive later are shown with a later
    frame, and of several ready results only the newest is drawn. The composite
    modes wait as the synchronous path did. When the render thread cannot
    start, the synchronous path renders as before.

    While paused no later frame comes. A frame shown while paused (a seek) has
    no queue lead to protect and waits like a redraw, and a result that still
    arrives after its frame wakes the VO thread, which redraws the frame on
    screen with it without filing a new request.

    The inline overlay now draws every item of a frame's list into one buffer;
    before, a second item (the OSD, a secondary subtitle) replaced the first.

* 0037-avfoundation-skip-redundant-per-frame-metadata:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:30:00 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: stop rewriting buffer metadata that already
     matches

    Two per-frame calls in create_video_sample_buffer() repeated work that
    was already done.

    tag_pixel_buffer_pixel_aspect() removed the pixel aspect attachment from
    every square-pixel frame. On a VideoToolbox passthrough buffer FFmpeg
    has just written the frame's sample aspect ratio there, 1:1 for a
    square-pixel stream, and removing an attachment from an IOSurface-backed
    buffer is a kernel round trip (0.9 ms of CPU per second of 4K playback).
    Read the attachment first and only write or remove it when its ratio
    differs from the one wanted; a missing attachment counts as square, and
    a 1:1 one now stays.

    The cached format description was checked with
    CMVideoFormatDescriptionMatchesImageBuffer() before every
    CMSampleBufferCreateReadyWithImageBuffer(), which runs the same check
    itself and fails with kCMSampleBufferError_InvalidMediaFormat on a
    mismatch (0.8 ms/s). Create the sample buffer against the cached
    description directly, and rebuild the description and retry only when
    that call refuses it.

* 0038-avfoundation-wait-for-renderer-readiness:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:00:00 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: wait for renderer readiness instead of
     polling it

    When the display layer's renderer was not ready for more media data,
    wait_until_ready_for_more_media_data() slept 1 ms at a time and asked
    isReadyForMoreMediaData again until the renderer was ready or the
    frame's deadline passed. About half the frames meet a full renderer: on
    a 4K HDR10 HEVC file the VO reported 56-72 ready-waits per 5 s averaging
    5.8-7.0 ms, so the VO thread woke about 80 times a second only to poll,
    and the player process ran at 136-147 wakeups/s where AVPlayer runs at
    31. At speed=2 nearly every frame waits, about 22 ms each, and the
    player ran at 828 wakeups/s.

    Arm requestMediaDataWhenReadyOnQueue: for the wait instead. The renderer
    runs the block once it is ready, at once if it already is, so readiness
    that arrives between the check and the request is not missed, and the
    block signals a semaphore the VO thread sleeps on. Each wait sleeps at
    most 20 ms and never past the frame's give-up time, and the loop
    re-checks readiness, recovery and the deadline after every wait as
    before, so deadline drops and layer recovery keep their behaviour.

    The block disarms the request itself, because a block that returns
    without enqueueing is run again at once (1.5 million times in 300 ms in
    a standalone test). stopRequestingMediaData drops the renderer's
    reference to the block while the block is still running, so a block
    only the renderer holds reads its captured semaphore from freed memory
    after the stop: a standalone loop doing that crashes in
    dispatch_semaphore_signal within a second. The wait therefore holds its
    own copy of the block, which runs on a private serial queue, and drains
    that queue after stopping the request before releasing the copy; that
    also covers an invocation that began just before a timed-out wait
    stopped the request. The same loop then ran 16,500 waits under
    MallocScribble and Guard Malloc without a fault.

    A renderer that fails without becoming ready need not run the block, so
    a wait can now reach the deadline without having seen a failure the 1
    ms poll would have caught. Check for a failed layer before declaring the
    deadline missed, so such a frame is recovered and enqueued rather than
    dropped.

    MPVBuildBench cell, 1280x720 pt window, 45 s measured, wakeups/s of the
    player process, every run without a dropped frame:

      hevc-4k-hdr10.mp4, 4 alternating pairs       136-147 -> 85.1-85.4
      hevc-4k-hdr10-90m.mp4 at speed=2                 828 -> 85

    With the patch the VO still reports 38-51 ready-waits per 5 s averaging
    6.2-7.7 ms on the first file, and 124-129 averaging 32-33 ms at speed=2,
    with no deadline drops.

* 0039-avfoundation-osd-layer-ignores-display-layer-transform:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:59:34 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: place the OSD layer from the display
     layer's untransformed geometry

    A host may zoom by scaling the display layer with a transform: mpv's
    --video-zoom sends every frame through the Core Image re-render, which
    breaks HDR and Dolby Vision passthrough. ensure_osd_layer() and
    sync_osd_layer_frame() copied the display layer's frame to the OSD
    layer, and under a transform that frame is the transformed bounding
    box, while the subtitle canvas is sized from the layer's bounds. The
    OSD layer stretched the unzoomed canvas over the zoomed box. At 120%
    zoom, subtitles came back 1.2x larger and scaled away from the centre,
    and a two-line subtitle lost its second line below the screen
    (plezy#2693). Any geometry sync while zoomed caused it: a VO recreated
    when the host re-enables video after the app was backgrounded (iOS
    lock and unlock), a rotation or window resize, or an aspect-override
    change.

    Place the OSD layer from the display layer's bounds, position and
    anchorPoint instead, which is its frame with the transform ignored, so
    the subtitles stay where mpv laid them out over the unzoomed picture.
    Without a transform the result equals the frame, so unzoomed hosts and
    the macOS PiP window, which receives both layers unzoomed, see no
    change. scripts/test_avf_osd_layer_geometry.sh compiles the helper and
    checks it against real Core Animation layers.

* 0040-avfoundation-dolby-vision-profile5-software-rpu:

    From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
    Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 02:28:47 +0200
    Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: give software-decoded Dolby Vision
     profile 5 frames their RPU

    Dolby Vision profile 5 played magenta and green on the iPad 10th and
    11th generation (plezy#2760). Those iPads have no Dolby Vision decoder:
    VTIsHardwareDecodeSupported(dvh1) is false, a dvh1 session fails with
    -12906, and AVPlayer cannot open the file. Profile 5 has no HDR10 base
    layer to fall back to, so it is decoded in software, and
    upload_software_frame() handed the display layer the raw full-range IPT
    codes in a video-range buffer with nothing to reshape them.

    The display pipeline does the reshaping itself when the frame carries
    its RPU, as a dvh1 session's output does, and it does so on these iPads
    as well. FFmpeg's HEVC decoder exports the RPU NAL payload as
    AV_FRAME_DATA_DOVI_RPU_BUFFER in the layout VideoToolbox attaches
    (dovi_tool reads the two as the same RPU; VideoToolbox only smooths the
    level 4 anchor). For a profile 5 frame, recognised the way
    ff_dovi_guess_profile_hevc() does, upload into a full-range 10-bit
    buffer and attach that payload as DolbyVisionRPUData. Profile 8,
    hardware frames and everything else are unchanged.

    Measured on AWS Device Farm with a profile 5 frame built into a pixel
    buffer and enqueued on an AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer, sRGB mean of a
    sand patch (reference, libplacebo's reshaping: 145,95,33):

      iPad (2025) A16, iPadOS 18.3.2, no DV decoder
        today's software upload                      245,0,106  magenta
        full-range codes + FFmpeg's RPU              198,128,53
      iPad (2022) A14, iPadOS 27.0, no DV decoder
        today's software upload                      199,0,84   magenta
        full-range codes + FFmpeg's RPU              160,102,38
      iPad Air 11 (2024) M2, iPadOS 26.1, DV decoder
        full-range codes + FFmpeg's RPU              160,102,38
        the layer decoding the file itself           160,102,38

    The A16's brighter result is iPadOS 18's tone mapping: an iPhone 11 on
    iOS 18.6.2, whose layer decodes the file itself, shows 198,128,52.
    Switching the layer to preferredDynamicRange .standard (an HDR toggle)
    or attaching VideoToolbox's copy of the RPU changed none of the values.

    End to end, the built libmpv playing the clip with vo=avfoundation,
    hwdec=videotoolbox and hwdec-software-fallback (Plezy's options), paused
    on the same frame, same patch:

      iPad (2025) A16, 18.3.2   software fallback        198,128,52
      iPad (2022) A14, 27.0     software fallback        160,102,38
      iPad Air 11 (2024), 26.1  videotoolbox, unchanged  160,102,38
                                hwdec=no                 160,102,38
      iPhone 14 Pro, 27.0       videotoolbox, unchanged  158,101,38
                                hwdec=no                 158,101,38

    scripts/test_avf_dovi_profile5_rpu.sh compiles the selector (the marked
    "avf dovi profile5 rpu" region) and checks that only profile 5 frames
    are picked.

